1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,003
Christmas, 1914. Two armies,

2
00:00:03,003 --> 00:00:04,403
dug into the frozen mud,

3
00:00:04,403 --> 00:00:06,157
trying to kill each other.

4
00:00:06,157 --> 00:00:07,357
Then, on Christmas Eve,

5
00:00:07,357 --> 00:00:09,111
the guns go quiet...

6
00:00:09,111 --> 00:00:10,963
and the enemies start to sing.

7
00:00:10,963 --> 00:00:13,107
By morning, they're shaking hands.

8
00:00:13,107 --> 00:00:14,707
So how did one of the deadliest

9
00:00:14,707 --> 00:00:16,147
wars in history stop,

10
00:00:16,147 --> 00:00:17,082
for Christmas?

11
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:21,660
Christmas Eve,

12
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:23,116
1914

13
00:00:23,116 --> 00:00:25,716
The First World War is only five months old

14
00:00:25,975 --> 00:00:25,975
Along the front lines in Belgium,

15
00:00:27,835 --> 00:00:30,075
British and German soldiers face each other

16
00:00:30,075 --> 00:00:31,803
across the frozen mud

17
00:00:31,803 --> 00:00:31,803
It's bitterly cold,

18
00:00:32,863 --> 00:00:34,783
and many of them are far from home for

19
00:00:34,783 --> 00:00:36,320
the very first time

20
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:36,320
For weeks,

21
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:38,936
they've been trying to kill each other

22
00:00:38,936 --> 00:00:38,936
But that night,

23
00:00:39,796 --> 00:00:41,676
something happens that no one could have

24
00:00:41,676 --> 00:00:42,608
predicted

25
00:00:42,608 --> 00:00:42,608
The shooting stops

26
00:00:44,170 --> 00:00:45,722
And the enemies...

27
00:00:45,722 --> 00:00:47,357
begin to sing

28
00:00:47,107 --> 00:00:47,357
This is the true story of the Christmas Truce

29
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,923
So how did it come to this?

30
00:00:55,923 --> 00:00:56,803
When the war began,

31
00:00:56,803 --> 00:00:58,723
in the summer of 1914,

32
00:00:58,723 --> 00:01:00,803
many people believed it would be over by

33
00:01:00,803 --> 00:01:01,923
Christmas

34
00:01:01,748 --> 00:01:02,018
Britain joined the war in August,

35
00:01:03,758 --> 00:01:05,518
to defend Belgium

36
00:01:05,390 --> 00:01:05,660
Young men marched off cheering,

37
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:09,405
with flowers in their caps

38
00:01:09,135 --> 00:01:09,405
Many had never left their home towns before

39
00:01:12,249 --> 00:01:12,729
For some,

40
00:01:12,729 --> 00:01:15,089
it felt like a great adventure

41
00:01:14,819 --> 00:01:15,089
But it wasn't

42
00:01:16,431 --> 00:01:18,628
The fighting quickly got stuck

43
00:01:18,358 --> 00:01:18,628
Huge battles in France and Belgium left

44
00:01:21,048 --> 00:01:23,648
hundreds of thousands of men killed or wounded

45
00:01:23,948 --> 00:01:24,218
So both sides started digging

46
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:27,580
Long lines of trenches,

47
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:29,660
across Belgium and France

48
00:01:29,507 --> 00:01:29,777
To survive the guns,

49
00:01:31,117 --> 00:01:33,717
soldiers had to dig deep into the ground

50
00:01:33,668 --> 00:01:33,938
The trenches stretched for hundreds of miles,

51
00:01:36,278 --> 00:01:38,878
from the sea all the way to Switzerland

52
00:01:38,888 --> 00:01:39,308
By December,

53
00:01:40,168 --> 00:01:41,273
it was clear

54
00:01:41,273 --> 00:01:43,873
Nobody was going home for Christmas

55
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:49,600
What was life like down there?

56
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:50,280
In a word:

57
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:51,400
miserable

58
00:01:51,266 --> 00:01:51,536
Many soldiers suffered from trench foot,

59
00:01:53,836 --> 00:01:56,436
from standing in freezing water for days

60
00:01:56,507 --> 00:01:56,777
It was cold and wet,

61
00:01:58,077 --> 00:01:59,357
the mud was everywhere,

62
00:01:59,357 --> 00:02:01,756
and rats ran through the dugouts

63
00:02:01,486 --> 00:02:01,756
Lice crawled in their clothes,

64
00:02:03,296 --> 00:02:05,444
and they could rarely wash

65
00:02:05,174 --> 00:02:05,444
Between the two lines lay a strip of torn-up

66
00:02:07,904 --> 00:02:10,221
ground called No Man's Land

67
00:02:09,951 --> 00:02:10,221
It was full of barbed wire,

68
00:02:11,761 --> 00:02:12,561
shell holes,

69
00:02:12,561 --> 00:02:13,836
and danger

70
00:02:13,566 --> 00:02:13,836
At night,

71
00:02:14,456 --> 00:02:17,016
soldiers crawled out to repair the barbed wire,

72
00:02:17,016 --> 00:02:19,099
hoping no one would see them

73
00:02:18,829 --> 00:02:19,099
And in some places,

74
00:02:20,239 --> 00:02:22,839
the enemy trenches were only fifty meters away

75
00:02:23,447 --> 00:02:23,717
Snipers watched for anyone who raised their

76
00:02:26,057 --> 00:02:27,472
head too high

77
00:02:27,202 --> 00:02:27,472
So close,

78
00:02:28,172 --> 00:02:30,772
that soldiers could hear each other talking

79
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:30,930
The British soldiers were called Tommies

80
00:02:33,189 --> 00:02:35,789
The Germans were often called Fritz

81
00:02:39,733 --> 00:02:41,093
As Christmas came closer,

82
00:02:41,093 --> 00:02:43,474
parcels started arriving from home

83
00:02:43,204 --> 00:02:43,474
Families sent socks,

84
00:02:44,814 --> 00:02:45,374
scarves,

85
00:02:45,374 --> 00:02:46,014
gloves,

86
00:02:46,014 --> 00:02:47,454
and cake

87
00:02:47,189 --> 00:02:47,459
British soldiers received small brass tins from

88
00:02:50,319 --> 00:02:51,239
Princess Mary,

89
00:02:51,239 --> 00:02:52,967
filled with treats

90
00:02:52,697 --> 00:02:52,967
Inside were tobacco or sweets,

91
00:02:54,867 --> 00:02:55,867
a Christmas card,

92
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:58,037
and a picture of the princess

93
00:02:57,767 --> 00:02:58,037
German soldiers were sent thousands of small

94
00:03:00,457 --> 00:03:01,897
Christmas trees

95
00:03:01,814 --> 00:03:02,084
Every British soldier also got a Christmas card

96
00:03:04,704 --> 00:03:06,475
from the King and Queen

97
00:03:06,205 --> 00:03:06,475
Even the Pope asked the armies to stop fighting

98
00:03:08,935 --> 00:03:10,302
for Christmas

99
00:03:10,032 --> 00:03:10,302
He hoped the guns would fall silent,

100
00:03:12,162 --> 00:03:14,762
at least on the night the angels sang

101
00:03:14,533 --> 00:03:14,803
The answer from the generals?

102
00:03:16,989 --> 00:03:17,779
No

103
00:03:17,779 --> 00:03:19,691
The war would go on

104
00:03:19,271 --> 00:03:19,691
The soldiers expected Christmas to be just like

105
00:03:22,431 --> 00:03:24,085
every other day of the war

106
00:03:24,085 --> 00:03:25,845
They were wrong

107
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:30,313
On Christmas Eve,

108
00:03:30,313 --> 00:03:32,787
British soldiers noticed something strange

109
00:03:32,787 --> 00:03:33,307
Lights,

110
00:03:33,307 --> 00:03:35,707
appearing along the German trenches

111
00:03:35,562 --> 00:03:35,832
The rain had stopped,

112
00:03:36,812 --> 00:03:39,303
and a hard frost covered the ground

113
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:39,303
At first,

114
00:03:39,843 --> 00:03:41,751
they thought it was a trick

115
00:03:41,481 --> 00:03:41,751
Officers warned their men to stay alert

116
00:03:44,414 --> 00:03:46,762
This could be the start of an attack

117
00:03:46,492 --> 00:03:46,762
But the lights were candles,

118
00:03:48,302 --> 00:03:50,862
on small Christmas trees placed along the top

119
00:03:50,862 --> 00:03:52,133
of the trench

120
00:03:51,863 --> 00:03:52,133
Paper lanterns and candles glowed in the

121
00:03:54,593 --> 00:03:55,193
darkness,

122
00:03:55,193 --> 00:03:57,037
all along the line

123
00:03:56,767 --> 00:03:57,037
And then...

124
00:03:58,382 --> 00:03:59,182
they heard singing,

125
00:03:59,182 --> 00:04:01,553
drifting across No Man's Land

126
00:04:01,283 --> 00:04:01,553
The sound carried clearly through the still,

127
00:04:03,693 --> 00:04:05,133
freezing air

128
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:05,250
It was a German carol

129
00:04:06,964 --> 00:04:08,283
Stille Nacht

130
00:04:08,283 --> 00:04:09,723
Silent Night

131
00:04:09,546 --> 00:04:09,966
Many British soldiers knew the tune

132
00:04:12,274 --> 00:04:14,354
They listened in silence

133
00:04:14,116 --> 00:04:14,386
Some of the German soldiers had worked in

134
00:04:16,566 --> 00:04:17,806
Britain before the war,

135
00:04:17,806 --> 00:04:20,406
and they called out in English

136
00:04:23,867 --> 00:04:25,705
So what did the British do?

137
00:04:25,705 --> 00:04:26,866
They listened

138
00:04:26,866 --> 00:04:28,599
And then they clapped

139
00:04:28,329 --> 00:04:28,599
The Germans cheered back

140
00:04:30,524 --> 00:04:30,794
And the British started singing carols of their

141
00:04:32,814 --> 00:04:33,729
own

142
00:04:33,459 --> 00:04:33,729
Songs like O Come,

143
00:04:34,829 --> 00:04:36,949
All Ye Faithful were sung by both sides,

144
00:04:36,949 --> 00:04:39,173
in English and in Latin

145
00:04:38,903 --> 00:04:39,173
Soon,

146
00:04:39,633 --> 00:04:41,433
the two sides were taking turns,

147
00:04:41,433 --> 00:04:43,673
singing back and forth across the frozen

148
00:04:43,673 --> 00:04:44,694
ground

149
00:04:44,424 --> 00:04:44,694
Some soldiers held up signs saying:

150
00:04:46,794 --> 00:04:48,234
Merry Christmas

151
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:48,470
Voices called out in the dark:

152
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:51,050
Merry Christmas,

153
00:04:51,050 --> 00:04:51,791
Tommy!

154
00:04:51,791 --> 00:04:52,791
Merry Christmas,

155
00:04:52,791 --> 00:04:53,911
Fritz!

156
00:04:53,683 --> 00:04:53,953
In some places,

157
00:04:55,013 --> 00:04:55,893
signs read:

158
00:04:55,893 --> 00:04:56,573
You no fight,

159
00:04:56,573 --> 00:04:57,947
we no fight

160
00:04:57,677 --> 00:04:57,947
For the first time in weeks,

161
00:04:59,487 --> 00:05:01,567
the guns were silent

162
00:05:01,179 --> 00:05:01,599
And in some places,

163
00:05:02,779 --> 00:05:05,059
officers met in No Man's Land that night,

164
00:05:05,059 --> 00:05:07,065
and agreed to a short truce

165
00:05:07,065 --> 00:05:09,185
But the most amazing part came the next

166
00:05:09,185 --> 00:05:10,305
morning

167
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:15,139
Christmas morning

168
00:05:15,139 --> 00:05:17,059
A few brave soldiers climbed out of their

169
00:05:17,059 --> 00:05:18,179
trenches

170
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:18,250
Everyone held their breath

171
00:05:20,098 --> 00:05:22,178
One shot could start the fighting all over

172
00:05:22,178 --> 00:05:23,157
again

173
00:05:22,887 --> 00:05:23,157
They walked into No Man's Land without their

174
00:05:25,417 --> 00:05:26,017
weapons,

175
00:05:26,017 --> 00:05:27,949
holding up their hands

176
00:05:27,679 --> 00:05:27,949
Others waved from the trenches,

177
00:05:29,809 --> 00:05:31,986
calling to them to come over

178
00:05:31,716 --> 00:05:31,986
Slowly,

179
00:05:32,606 --> 00:05:33,286
men stood up,

180
00:05:33,286 --> 00:05:35,162
and climbed out to meet them

181
00:05:34,892 --> 00:05:35,162
And nobody fired

182
00:05:36,814 --> 00:05:37,234
The two sides met in the middle

183
00:05:39,214 --> 00:05:40,583
A little nervous

184
00:05:40,583 --> 00:05:42,128
A little shy

185
00:05:41,858 --> 00:05:42,128
More and more men followed,

186
00:05:43,588 --> 00:05:45,348
from both sides

187
00:05:45,090 --> 00:05:45,360
Many Germans spoke some English,

188
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:49,780
so the men talked with words and gestures

189
00:05:49,795 --> 00:05:50,065
Soon,

190
00:05:50,605 --> 00:05:52,565
hundreds of enemy soldiers were standing

191
00:05:52,565 --> 00:05:53,165
together,

192
00:05:53,165 --> 00:05:54,605
shaking hands

193
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:54,920
Even officers from both sides joined in,

194
00:05:57,380 --> 00:05:59,140
saluting each other

195
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:03,480
And then,

196
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:05,880
the soldiers started swapping gifts

197
00:06:05,923 --> 00:06:06,193
British soldiers gave away jam and corned beef

198
00:06:09,269 --> 00:06:11,864
The Germans offered beer and schnapps

199
00:06:11,594 --> 00:06:11,864
Chocolate,

200
00:06:12,444 --> 00:06:13,084
cigarettes,

201
00:06:13,084 --> 00:06:13,684
sausages,

202
00:06:13,684 --> 00:06:15,028
plum pudding...

203
00:06:15,028 --> 00:06:17,401
even buttons from their uniforms

204
00:06:17,131 --> 00:06:17,401
Some swapped caps,

205
00:06:18,541 --> 00:06:20,446
to keep as souvenirs

206
00:06:20,176 --> 00:06:20,446
They showed each other photos of their

207
00:06:22,386 --> 00:06:22,906
families,

208
00:06:22,906 --> 00:06:24,346
and wrote down addresses,

209
00:06:24,346 --> 00:06:26,644
promising to meet after the war

210
00:06:26,374 --> 00:06:26,644
Many of them were fathers,

211
00:06:28,104 --> 00:06:30,704
missing their own children back home

212
00:06:30,464 --> 00:06:30,734
In one place,

213
00:06:31,434 --> 00:06:33,834
a German soldier who'd been a barber before the

214
00:06:33,834 --> 00:06:36,234
war gave British soldiers haircuts

215
00:06:36,157 --> 00:06:36,427
Others laughed,

216
00:06:37,327 --> 00:06:38,087
sang songs,

217
00:06:38,087 --> 00:06:40,157
and took photographs together

218
00:06:39,887 --> 00:06:40,157
Just one day before,

219
00:06:41,377 --> 00:06:43,311
they had been enemies

220
00:06:42,891 --> 00:06:43,311
Now they were sharing Christmas in the middle

221
00:06:45,371 --> 00:06:47,131
of a battlefield

222
00:06:50,833 --> 00:06:51,953
And in some places,

223
00:06:51,953 --> 00:06:54,190
something even more amazing appeared

224
00:06:54,190 --> 00:06:55,620
A football

225
00:06:55,350 --> 00:06:55,620
Nobody knows exactly where it started,

226
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:00,120
but soldiers wrote about it in their letters

227
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:01,005
home

228
00:07:00,735 --> 00:07:01,005
Men started kicking it around on the frozen,

229
00:07:03,505 --> 00:07:04,945
muddy ground

230
00:07:04,819 --> 00:07:05,089
Officers and privates played side by side,

231
00:07:07,549 --> 00:07:10,011
in heavy boots and thick coats

232
00:07:09,741 --> 00:07:10,011
Caps and helmets became goalposts

233
00:07:12,524 --> 00:07:12,794
One German officer wrote that the British

234
00:07:14,974 --> 00:07:16,414
brought a ball from their trench,

235
00:07:16,414 --> 00:07:18,246
and a game began

236
00:07:17,976 --> 00:07:18,246
In some games,

237
00:07:19,146 --> 00:07:21,746
dozens of men chased the ball at once

238
00:07:21,652 --> 00:07:21,922
There were no real teams

239
00:07:23,645 --> 00:07:25,405
Nobody kept score

240
00:07:25,236 --> 00:07:25,506
Some players slipped and fell in the mud,

241
00:07:27,526 --> 00:07:29,286
and everyone laughed

242
00:07:29,130 --> 00:07:29,400
Just young men,

243
00:07:30,340 --> 00:07:31,260
far from home,

244
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:33,860
playing a game together on Christmas Day

245
00:07:33,923 --> 00:07:34,343
For a few hours,

246
00:07:35,363 --> 00:07:37,963
there was no war at all

247
00:07:40,933 --> 00:07:42,975
So how big was the truce?

248
00:07:42,975 --> 00:07:44,309
It wasn't planned

249
00:07:44,309 --> 00:07:46,189
It started with ordinary soldiers,

250
00:07:46,189 --> 00:07:48,627
in many places along the line

251
00:07:48,357 --> 00:07:48,627
It happened mostly between British and German

252
00:07:51,127 --> 00:07:51,607
troops,

253
00:07:51,607 --> 00:07:53,966
near the Belgian town of Ypres

254
00:07:53,696 --> 00:07:53,966
There was no single truce,

255
00:07:55,506 --> 00:07:56,746
but many small ones,

256
00:07:56,746 --> 00:07:58,828
each starting in its own way

257
00:07:58,558 --> 00:07:58,828
Historians think around one hundred thousand

258
00:08:01,408 --> 00:08:02,839
men took part

259
00:08:03,019 --> 00:08:05,019
French and Belgian soldiers joined in,

260
00:08:05,019 --> 00:08:07,089
in a few places too

261
00:08:06,819 --> 00:08:07,089
In some places,

262
00:08:08,109 --> 00:08:10,160
it lasted only a few hours

263
00:08:10,160 --> 00:08:10,760
In others,

264
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:13,003
it lasted until the New Year

265
00:08:12,733 --> 00:08:13,003
In one place,

266
00:08:13,863 --> 00:08:16,103
chaplains from both sides held a service

267
00:08:16,103 --> 00:08:16,623
together,

268
00:08:16,623 --> 00:08:19,223
reading a psalm in English and German

269
00:08:19,024 --> 00:08:19,294
Both sides also used the quiet time to bury

270
00:08:21,994 --> 00:08:23,234
their fallen friends,

271
00:08:23,234 --> 00:08:25,314
sometimes side by side

272
00:08:25,151 --> 00:08:25,381
Back home,

273
00:08:26,041 --> 00:08:28,201
newspapers printed soldiers' letters,

274
00:08:28,201 --> 00:08:30,612
and even photos of the truce

275
00:08:30,342 --> 00:08:30,612
It was a moment of peace in the middle

276
00:08:32,512 --> 00:08:34,373
of a terrible war

277
00:08:33,953 --> 00:08:34,373
Many soldiers wrote that they would never

278
00:08:36,313 --> 00:08:37,753
forget it

279
00:08:41,367 --> 00:08:43,198
But not everyone was happy

280
00:08:43,198 --> 00:08:45,158
When the generals heard about the truce,

281
00:08:45,158 --> 00:08:46,866
they were furious

282
00:08:46,596 --> 00:08:46,866
To them,

283
00:08:47,366 --> 00:08:49,966
the soldiers had forgotten who the enemy was

284
00:08:50,077 --> 00:08:50,347
They worried that soldiers who made friends

285
00:08:52,367 --> 00:08:54,835
with the enemy would refuse to fight

286
00:08:54,565 --> 00:08:54,835
Some officers feared the men would start asking

287
00:08:57,335 --> 00:08:59,261
why they were fighting at all

288
00:08:58,991 --> 00:08:59,261
New orders came down

289
00:09:01,051 --> 00:09:03,651
Making friends with the enemy was forbidden

290
00:09:03,782 --> 00:09:04,052
In some places,

291
00:09:05,072 --> 00:09:07,392
soldiers fired warning shots into the air,

292
00:09:07,392 --> 00:09:09,992
to tell their new friends the truce was over

293
00:09:10,150 --> 00:09:10,420
Anyone who kept it up could be punished

294
00:09:12,879 --> 00:09:13,149
Units that had made friends were sometimes

295
00:09:15,529 --> 00:09:17,905
moved to other parts of the front

296
00:09:17,635 --> 00:09:17,905
Officers who had joined in were warned they

297
00:09:20,045 --> 00:09:22,099
could face serious trouble

298
00:09:21,829 --> 00:09:22,099
Soon,

299
00:09:22,519 --> 00:09:24,599
the shooting started again

300
00:09:24,293 --> 00:09:24,713
Within days,

301
00:09:25,613 --> 00:09:26,613
the war was back,

302
00:09:26,613 --> 00:09:29,013
as if nothing had happened

303
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:34,297
Did it ever happen again?

304
00:09:34,297 --> 00:09:35,297
The next Christmas,

305
00:09:35,297 --> 00:09:36,537
in 1915,

306
00:09:36,537 --> 00:09:39,107
there were only a few small truces

307
00:09:38,837 --> 00:09:39,107
Commanders ordered heavy shooting on Christmas

308
00:09:41,447 --> 00:09:41,727
Eve,

309
00:09:41,727 --> 00:09:43,756
to stop any friendship

310
00:09:43,486 --> 00:09:43,756
They made sure nothing like 1914 would happen

311
00:09:46,816 --> 00:09:47,737
again

312
00:09:47,467 --> 00:09:47,737
As the war dragged on,

313
00:09:49,077 --> 00:09:50,637
it grew more and more bitter,

314
00:09:50,637 --> 00:09:53,237
with poison gas and huge battles

315
00:09:53,111 --> 00:09:53,381
By 1916,

316
00:09:54,641 --> 00:09:56,801
after the giant battles of Verdun and the

317
00:09:56,801 --> 00:09:57,081
Somme,

318
00:09:57,081 --> 00:09:59,496
there were almost no truces at all

319
00:09:59,226 --> 00:09:59,496
The war went on for almost four more years

320
00:10:02,124 --> 00:10:02,394
It finally ended on November 11th,

321
00:10:04,654 --> 00:10:05,774
1918

322
00:10:06,147 --> 00:10:06,417
The cost was staggering

323
00:10:08,152 --> 00:10:09,592
Millions died

324
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:09,850
Around nine million of them were soldiers

325
00:10:12,488 --> 00:10:12,758
Many of the men who shook hands that Christmas

326
00:10:15,178 --> 00:10:16,946
never made it home

327
00:10:16,476 --> 00:10:16,946
But those who survived never forgot that

328
00:10:19,246 --> 00:10:20,366
Christmas

329
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:24,680
Today,

330
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:27,120
the Christmas Truce is remembered as one of the

331
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:29,345
most human moments of the war

332
00:10:29,075 --> 00:10:29,345
It's been told in books,

333
00:10:30,844 --> 00:10:31,284
songs,

334
00:10:31,284 --> 00:10:31,685
films,

335
00:10:31,685 --> 00:10:33,765
and even TV adverts

336
00:10:33,957 --> 00:10:35,397
Memorials stand in Belgium,

337
00:10:35,397 --> 00:10:37,387
near where the soldiers met

338
00:10:37,117 --> 00:10:37,387
A football memorial was unveiled there in 2014,

339
00:10:40,487 --> 00:10:42,567
for the hundredth anniversary

340
00:10:42,481 --> 00:10:42,751
Every year,

341
00:10:43,411 --> 00:10:44,891
visitors leave footballs,

342
00:10:44,891 --> 00:10:47,479
crosses and poppies at the memorial

343
00:10:47,209 --> 00:10:47,479
In 2014,

344
00:10:48,619 --> 00:10:49,899
one hundred years later,

345
00:10:49,899 --> 00:10:52,459
the British and German armies played a football

346
00:10:52,459 --> 00:10:54,115
match to remember it

347
00:10:53,845 --> 00:10:54,115
The British Army won,

348
00:10:55,295 --> 00:10:56,940
one goal to nil

349
00:10:56,670 --> 00:10:56,940
It showed that even in the middle of a

350
00:10:58,760 --> 00:10:59,120
war,

351
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:01,200
people can choose kindness

352
00:11:01,014 --> 00:11:01,284
Ordinary people on both sides found they had

353
00:11:03,784 --> 00:11:06,103
far more in common than they thought

354
00:11:05,833 --> 00:11:06,103
Soldiers' letters are kept in museums,

355
00:11:08,323 --> 00:11:10,923
so we can still read their words today

356
00:11:10,697 --> 00:11:10,967
For one silent night,

357
00:11:12,067 --> 00:11:13,827
enemies chose peace

358
00:11:15,033 --> 00:11:16,486
One silent night

359
00:11:16,486 --> 00:11:18,008
Two enemy armies

360
00:11:18,008 --> 00:11:19,655
One game of football

361
00:11:19,605 --> 00:11:19,655
No orders

362
00:11:20,958 --> 00:11:22,062
No plan

363
00:11:22,062 --> 00:11:24,107
Just people choosing peace

364
00:11:24,007 --> 00:11:24,107
Would you have climbed out of the trench?

365
00:11:26,360 --> 00:11:26,410
Tell me in the comments

366
00:11:28,045 --> 00:11:28,095
And subscribe,

367
00:11:28,995 --> 00:11:31,595
so you don't miss the next story
